steps.body:
A `struct` groups related values into one new type. Each value is a *field* (also called a member). Note the semicolon after the closing brace: forgetting it gives a confusing error on the next line.

```cpp
struct Point {
    int x = 0;   // default member initializer
    int y = 0;
};

Point p{3, 4};             // aggregate initialization, in field order
std::cout << p.x << '\n';  // 3, member access with .
p.y = 10;
Point origin;              // x and y use their defaults
std::cout << origin.x << ' ' << origin.y << '\n';  // 0 0
```

Braces fill the fields in declaration order, and any field you leave out takes its default. Without a default, an `int` or `double` field of a plain `Point origin;` would hold garbage, so give those fields one. A `std::string` field starts empty on its own.

A struct works like any other type. `std::vector<Point>` holds many of them, and `pts.push_back({1, 2})` builds a temporary `Point` from the braces and stores it. `pts.emplace_back(1, 2)` does not compile here, because in C++17 `emplace_back` needs a constructor and `Point` has none. Structs can be large, so pass them to functions by `const Point&` to read them without a copy.

example.code:
#include <iomanip>
#include <iostream>
#include <string>
#include <vector>

struct Book {
    std::string title;
    int pages = 0;
    double price = 0.0;
};

void printBook(const Book& b) {
    std::cout << b.title << ": " << b.pages << " pages, $" << b.price << '\n';
}

int main() {
    std::cout << std::fixed << std::setprecision(2);
    Book dune{"Dune", 412, 9.99};
    dune.price = 8.99;

    std::vector<Book> shelf;
    shelf.push_back(dune);
    shelf.push_back({"Emma", 474, 7.50});
    shelf.push_back({"Hamlet", 104});

    int totalPages = 0;
    for (const auto& b : shelf) {
        printBook(b);
        totalPages += b.pages;
    }
    std::cout << "Total pages: " << totalPages << '\n';
    return 0;
}

