steps.body:
A struct can hold functions too. A *member function* (method) is defined inside the struct and called on an object with `obj.method()`. Inside it, the fields are used directly by name: they belong to whichever object the call was made on.

```cpp
struct Rectangle {
    double width = 0.0;
    double height = 0.0;

    double area() const {
        return width * height;
    }
    void scale(double factor) {
        width *= factor;
        height *= factor;
    }
};
Rectangle r{3.0, 4.0};
std::cout << r.area() << '\n';  // 12
```

`const` after the parameter list promises the method does not change the object. The compiler enforces it, and only const methods can be called through a `const Rectangle&`. So mark every method that only reads.

Every method receives a hidden pointer named `this` that points at the object. `width` inside a method is shorthand for `this->width`. The `->` operator reaches a member through a pointer: `p->area()` means `(*p).area()`.

